field/id #uuid "64e92ee8-c463-5350-91b5-0bace844e68b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289946}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "122300d6-48f4-52e5-9b3e-da4687523cb3" :question/math-academy-id "q-23216" :question/problem "The point $A$ is located in the first quadrant and lies on the unit circle. The $x$-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{1}{4}.$ What is the $y$-coordinate of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the point $A$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem: \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ into the above and solving for $y$ gives,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{16}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1-\\frac{1}{16} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{15}{16} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{\\frac{15}{16}} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ lies in the first quadrant, $y$ must be positive, so we select the positive answer. Therefore, our final answer is $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335778 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05b822d1-8ee0-58a2-93ff-cb187dfa5db0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333039} {:answer/id #uuid "172c2164-8a29-5506-a361-8ccd4decba98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{21}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333196} {:answer/id #uuid "5025acf5-7673-5024-a61f-a855686d95e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333720} {:answer/id #uuid "d263b596-7ef8-56b5-a591-3fd063e528a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334827} {:answer/id #uuid "e6630e6d-1da4-555c-a3f5-d70e2b45b53e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{21}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335000}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5025acf5-7673-5024-a61f-a855686d95e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333720} :answer-field/id #uuid "9047e8c0-5f50-5691-b54b-073e8bf98b88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335514}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "efddc82f-972e-5912-af23-4395e0d8ee20" :question/math-academy-id "q-65031" :question/problem "The point $A$ is located in the first quadrant and lies on the unit circle. The $x$-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{2}{5}.$ What is the $y$-coordinate of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the point $A$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem: \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{2}{5}$ into the above and solving for $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {(\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4}{25}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1-\\frac{4}{25} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{21}{25} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{\\frac{21}{25}} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ lies in the first quadrant, $y$ must be positive, so we select the positive answer. Therefore, our final answer is $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{5}.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Pythagorean Theorem on the Unit Circle"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110755 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110457 :question/id #uuid "02c747c2-0eda-5c53-8e43-829f878dd278" :question/math-academy-id "e-3482" :question/problem "![](images/aa/aad0b38f5afb88c13039ac577a927e232d84905de40297ed824c966795ce7bb4.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\dfrac{2}{3},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin\\theta.$" :question/worked-solution "Any point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\dfrac{2}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{2}{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{4}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{5}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $\\sin\\theta$ is positive in the first quadrant. \n\nSo our final answer is $\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sqrt 5}{3}.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c73db44-2ec2-5025-9431-acb02e414916" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050291 :topic/math-academy-id 112 :topic/title "Properties of the Unit Circle in the First Quadrant"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111158 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b2b084c-2030-569c-b3db-c1f0bb240497" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108674} {:answer/id #uuid "a1178c63-4702-5bdc-9e41-c3046acb6f63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108675} {:answer/id #uuid "d3dca092-57eb-5e07-8d0d-0d53376fcddf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108676} {:answer/id #uuid "ec1a8afd-47ad-5ee5-aef2-253b8da8405f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108677} {:answer/id #uuid "f8b6b5a3-b768-5bb1-8d7e-9d7a7b3b6af1" :answer/type {:db/id