e point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$, use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\cos θ$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259697 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1899aeff-6c99-5c03-9546-b76ef39af986" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256995} {:answer/id #uuid "3d869581-ecbb-520d-8a4e-930bb77c4ffd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257329} {:answer/id #uuid "6473b1ab-aae1-5e0b-a569-58d6da31fd9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257665} {:answer/id #uuid "8fc46843-1ec1-59a1-bcce-8c1afc2c6e65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258046} {:answer/id #uuid "a6cbcfd0-4ae8-5672-951c-dc2e216fa8f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258238}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6cbcfd0-4ae8-5672-951c-dc2e216fa8f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258238} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2385214-bc0d-583b-b908-7acf965a5700" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259317}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "371bdeca-3a35-5956-9fa6-e8d03505d26b" :question/math-academy-id "q-124625" :question/problem "![](images/e5/e50a755a20330d7f0eca8d02c8ddab33f392d5b70f7ca35b4dc514e22e61141c.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{6}{7},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/fb/fb0da765a857607ce0c8f13690efaa2f0d504ccaa51899a23044ee4ed17f0aa5.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{6}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡\\theta =\\frac{6}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{6}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{6}{7})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{36}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{13}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So our final answer is $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{7}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323955 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a4cafde-db4c-54bb-bab3-013f762c6d9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321731} {:answer/id #uuid "4e99ea8e-9a16-57a0-baea-c6ae781ca29a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321765} {:answer/id #uuid "5cf875a9-930b-5577-b821-7ead99c40d06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321884} {:answer/id #uuid "7fe73b65-ebc3-5cff-9a52-a69da7741fb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322150} {:answer/id #uuid "b124652f-7ab9-5b4c-9413-d3dfdcb4c6a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322535}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e99ea8e-9a16-57a0-baea-c6ae781ca29a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321765} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce29bc07-9e48-58fd-9669-3eeb517f79e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323572}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "565688d3-a42b-5b17-9265-a372de4b8754" :question/math-academy-id "q-37899" :question/problem "![](images/f4/f4fdf6f8d819a4ee043b1cc3b86d795e3166ebfe181e0958246dc084c2f9c6fc.png)\n\nThe point $P$ lies on the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of $P$ is $\\frac{3}{7},$ use the Pythagorean Identity to find $sin⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/cb/cb569d4b7b76699259d2167b8c930e636e3fb794b14ef25a4d909c224235d7c3.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{3}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{3}{7})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{9}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{40}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So our final answer is $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{10}}{7}$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323957 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d8126b5-2054-5d88-865f-be6cbe674d45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321368} {:answer/id #uuid "3466518b-1130-594a-9f91-8439ec16fa46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321544} {:answer/id #uuid "51eee8c3-23ea-5613-a452-4c2182222cb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321794} {:answer/id #uuid "5ba4b8d7-052a-5cf2-86db-50636fe22d83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321871} {:answer/id #uuid "c2131ada-4ea2-5f29-85f1-aa42dc442a53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322653}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3466518b-1130-594a-9f91-8439ec16fa46" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321544} :answer-field/id #uuid "617b107f-dc16-5899-aab5-ab0f5850cc8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323364}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9112180a-ac6b-5472-ba8b-819d346ead1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-37915" :question/problem "![](images/6c/6c36dbce847c719accc77fa15f33dfb9a87cfae669691afe30f7693663076d2e.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/52/52fe94bb303a001e89f9db221e9730d4ca8c5c284217aa20fb6084ee041dec66.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{(\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =2-2(\\frac{10}{16}) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{20}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our final answer is $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335735 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d900898-f644-59cb-8bd9-292f8cf96161" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333108} {:answer/id #uuid "128843bf-c0d0-5b0b-9af7-5c0891c02535" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333155} {:answer/id #uuid "867d9b75-2324-5dfe-830f-942376886bea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334183} {:answer/id #uuid "de2d463a-06e0-5a98-b913-8a3a09131403" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334926} {:answer/id #uuid "f58082e0-41ed-5179-bfb1-b391fbd71bba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335143}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "867d9b75-2324-5dfe-830f-942376886bea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334183} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4eb5247-b68b-5a82-8180-4c0ffe3de0aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335575}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e9c7086f-08af-5d07-8de2-8cc3da9eb077" :question/math-academy-id "q-64681" :question/problem "![](images/94/94b8650466747f6858b30adbb8962e6c91159466b570f536d4999e2b08c31b67.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{1}{2},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/cb/cb569d4b7b76699259d2167b8c930e636e3fb794b14ef25a4d909c224235d7c3.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So our final answer is $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335783 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ebb8345-8448-5af3-a7b5-f875fc57e26d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{55}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333119} {:answer/id #uuid "87f8bdf1-b693-5449-95dc-41601ab98dee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334192} {:answer/id #uuid "9cd4a426-2676-5c7e-b66d-c6dd8478bed9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{56}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334369} {:answer/id #uuid "ac5680a5-b135-5e11-a025-14cbaf19a181" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{14}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334503} {:answer/id #uuid "af99c88e-1b1e-54e6-8e94-cc6dcf9705d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{56}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334536}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac5680a5-b135-5e11-a025-14cbaf19a181" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{14}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334503} :answer-field/id #uuid "2831461c-74ea-58e4-8c19-3e42bd31b998" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335313}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "43fa1511-b2d2-52bf-92cb-53a58f4a78b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-65059" :question/problem "![](images/5d/5d292a3598346bd05511c9c8979ba42845a88afe75a4740d8c4bdb9aa3968c87.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{5}{9},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/48/488ff32187c9a9a335e3782fb717242d899f622c43010662e28edcabe0e1bde3.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{5}{9}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{9}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{9}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{5}{9})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{25}{81}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{56}{81}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{14}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So our final answer is $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{14}}{9}.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110756 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110458 :question/id #uuid "bbf71023-510c-54af-9d0c-4df6744e3007" :question/math-academy-id "e-3484" :question/problem "![](images/e5/e50848432dd18a4a5dc64d4e5a5cfb8b3e14e9fed6231774dd83ead5552cd463.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\dfrac{2\\sqrt 6}{7},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\csc\\theta.$" :question/worked-solution "Again, any point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx = \\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = \\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\dfrac{2\\sqrt 6}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{2\\sqrt 6}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{2\\sqrt 6}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{24}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{25}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{5}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\sin\\theta$ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{5}{7}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\csc\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\sin\\theta},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\csc\\theta = \\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{5}{7}\\right)} = \\dfrac{7}{5}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "435bc6f9-651f-511c-a972-9afe90baff32" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048702 :topic/math-academy-id 1318 :topic/title "Rationalizing Denominators"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050928 :topic/math-academy-id 611 :topic/title "The Reciprocal Trigonometric Ratios"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051029 :topic/math-academy-id 323 :topic/title "Fractions of Fractions"}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110900 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "221a4245-8d36-54ef-a82d-e80ee76d86ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9\\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257060} {:answer/id #uuid "77312d37-9886-5569-95f9-c48a271a75f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{6}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257837} {:answer/id #uuid "8319e688-c42a-58a8-aa2e-6c419db98208" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257942} {:answer/id #uuid "aa4aecba-9357-5bb7-978f-a673979c6b60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{6}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258268} {:answer/id #uuid "b24f29ad-ae26-5c1a-98cc-8488a4660ab6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\sqrt{6}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258335}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa4aecba-9357-5bb7-978f-a673979c6b60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{6}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258268} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ed6599f-2974-5160-b981-962e1c285d91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259194}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2755d3ad-f502-5322-8d2b-3addfcafe3f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-124750" :question/problem "![](images/bd/bd8650a856fe3136759d81a886b2d4622cf3eb463b75e6023b44083c72f63a1a.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{1}{5},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $sec⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/73/734eef45985ddfb6450666e1dd32c6d3beab9ca3aef66c87c6ba393d6960b08b.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{1}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{24}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{5}.$\n\nKnowing that $sec⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{5})}=\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{6}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2\\sqrt{6}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{6}}{2\\cdot 6} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{6}}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111341 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9081c344-3a39-5a06-bd28-8b285c2ea9a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109772} {:answer/id #uuid "93524fd2-35f0-5d71-8d9e-e239e386e599" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109773} {:answer/id #uuid "a262a04e-d822-56f0-a70e-27321fda9467" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109774} {:answer/id #uuid "f7b263b5-8f52-5c67-8e20-36803479f595" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109775} {:answer/id #uuid "5e75bdf0-de4a-526f-95f5-f7ef9a319ea5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109776}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2e52fa46-45c4-5057-a515-a4ac0eafd22d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109771}] :question/id #uuid "42d69a07-5334-57e8-884c-ddd2699ea549" :question/math-academy-id "q-65066" :question/problem "![](images/e6/e666ddc8a8eda452abd833e963446c96de5d34dfbf76e9c92564e85a48080524.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{3}{5}$, use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sec θ$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259728 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ae50b4c-8d48-5e94-905d-2050bbd834e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258142} {:answer/id #uuid "a5ae39b9-8d77-5506-95cb-36cfda6b93ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258226} {:answer/id #uuid "b1ca8282-18dd-559f-b3d0-161a4153c226" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258329} {:answer/id #uuid "f50445f6-c5b6-5037-8d82-ae09d76d2629" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258921} {:answer/id #uuid "f57c9ed2-c68c-53ab-85fa-4cb0d40fff12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258925}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ae50b4c-8d48-5e94-905d-2050bbd834e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258142} :answer-field/id #uuid "62707eaf-bd66-53e1-b24c-8b731568b056" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259199}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "186cddc1-d1ed-556b-9213-9d3efc27c116" :question/math-academy-id "q-124743" :question/problem "![](images/02/02cb693b74f0f315389f87ceed1674da11f661b3363e4df0890bdf82d61cc60c.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{1}{3},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $csc⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/a4/a479e8e7906a3a0dc43e925c5bccd6534b4ab2e02a289403b2d0c92d0b0eb1d9.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{8}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3}.$\n\nKnowing that $csc⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{sin⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ncsc⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3})}=\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}csc⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2\\cdot 2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335785 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "596dbc8f-087c-5cf2-accd-e0290a3590a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333808} {:answer/id #uuid "ad4202f9-1df8-5702-aa30-7ebfe4130dbb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334517} {:answer/id #uuid "c29b3ce4-5742-5c0e-a80c-c46e912aa977" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334679} {:answer/id #uuid "cc5de6f1-fb22-59a3-a646-77d3c44a4d2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334771} {:answer/id #uuid "d1f58498-935a-53c3-af47-c294d082ad34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334822}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d1f58498-935a-53c3-af47-c294d082ad34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334822} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e139d51-01cf-5ad5-af15-a0777e8b7fe3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335266}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ee81fb0a-2d3c-5307-89ae-7908973bbefc" :question/math-academy-id "q-65062" :question/problem "![](images/43/43629ebfaa2f9811135ffb15f5e5a59b796b83e3a525b4ab480efb9840d40879.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $sec⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/f5/f5219a363cdf18cfb628148ca7b9bbe6015b521799ed4487fa9b56843eaa3043.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{3}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{6}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}.$\n\nKnowing that $sec⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3})}=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{6}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{6}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335786 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ffeba9c-70a6-5328-9dc3-5020e0313ad2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333595} {:answer/id #uuid "4eafe18b-8c02-5577-a960-94ba6f971ec7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333711} {:answer/id #uuid "7213fed3-dd70-59f5-9da6-eb714e406490" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334021} {:answer/id #uuid "9dc0b081-37ac-56aa-8ceb-99b894130c9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334379} {:answer/id #uuid "fdb4516a-8d82-5f18-ae27-7aaf1618cf06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335220}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ffeba9c-70a6-5328-9dc3-5020e0313ad2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333595} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0192dd7-c731-5b69-8d4c-3c9bab7026de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335562}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "555c1e17-369e-580d-8f06-901f476b3eee" :question/math-academy-id "q-65064" :question/problem "![](images/45/455df038e550d78fc29463c1a2e22dd44838d24af7428a824f9046fecc1ac1db.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{5},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $csc⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/44/44a555d222e3f6ff85208e8722a728ad240c558de7982d2a480bf9ddfb23ac6f.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{15}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{10}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5}.$\n\nKnowing that $csc⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{sin⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}csc⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5})}=\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{10}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}csc⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{10}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{10}}{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354407 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "528a08e6-b114-52bc-80b8-70909e3bcf6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352305} {:answer/id #uuid "840edc39-ce69-5e96-a971-721e9703e6b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352722} {:answer/id #uuid "9b103a08-6e43-5cc7-a32e-70ec6d35d281" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{7}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352896} {:answer/id #uuid "d780a273-2e9f-579d-b951-eb5dfc8fc835" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353437} {:answer/id #uuid "e9803a68-f2b3-5f47-b6d4-6601df336f7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d780a273-2e9f-579d-b951-eb5dfc8fc835" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353437} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1301c22-4c52-5421-ba45-744724340b9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354148}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5348ffc1-6c19-57de-8e72-a13dbe8b5969" :question/math-academy-id "q-85234" :question/problem "![](images/87/87635426224ded01c8bf486c7f59eeb6cacbe4e5c7db964643910bb5d142f0f3.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $sec⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/9f/9f64bae56998bca7d825780c8c2b437ae3106486053cc9c8ed1b1fca2a88d4ab.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{2}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{7}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3}.$\n\nKnowing that $sec⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{3})}=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{7}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{7}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{7}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{\\sqrt{7}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Reciprocal Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110758 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110459 :question/id #uuid "ba6cc6a0-dbb8-58d2-9d02-c76310028d4e" :question/math-academy-id "e-3485" :question/problem "![](images/e6/e666ddc8a8eda452abd833e963446c96de5d34dfbf76e9c92564e85a48080524.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\dfrac{4}{5},$ what is $\\tan\\theta?$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that $x=\\dfrac{4}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\sin\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\cos\\theta = \\dfrac{4}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\sin\\theta$ is positive in the first quadrant. Therefore $\\sin\\theta = \\dfrac{3}{5}.$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\sin\\theta}{\\cos\\theta},\n$$\n\nwe finally get\n\n$$\n\\tan\\theta = \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{3}{5}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{4}{5}\\right)} = \\dfrac {3}{4}.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74d05ad6-7e4a-5c50-92d1-ca4b2f00cbb1" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111339 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d58f0d3a-48cb-5dc6-87a4-afe1cfebcbfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109760} {:answer/id #uuid "db8dde9f-3c21-548d-8ee7-573fad3133d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109761} {:answer/id #uuid "de137338-a214-5e28-b644-3e94e4f16484" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109762} {:answer/id #uuid "08b8a4ea-1821-5bba-8395-25d188efb2cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109763} {:answer/id #uuid "152363af-0269-58b7-b058-87a6b12cc37c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109764}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a416b38a-3b4c-5694-8ff4-47b0c91eb2c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186109759}] :question/id #uuid "8a1f92d7-1b72-57f9-b8bc-455d27a59aca" :question/math-academy-id "q-64974" :question/problem "![](images/1b/1b084e0ad229e935d70d751f0e0ddaacf5832017951a7ef8e334f27834662d5c.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{2}{3}$, use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\cot θ$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111342 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f5011e1-5a24-51a6-a560-84948c0866a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333123} {:answer/id #uuid "1a630eb5-70c5-5ded-bed1-6c97af35f229" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{45}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333231} {:answer/id #uuid "72e4ea13-feef-5acf-9b9a-0e579d2765d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334027} {:answer/id #uuid "c360afef-02b3-5232-b951-ae761d89057d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334684} {:answer/id #uuid "fc79cebb-683f-5ec6-b279-a40dd479a779" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335208}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72e4ea13-feef-5acf-9b9a-0e579d2765d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334027} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a1eb48a-b984-5dff-bbdc-6a2962f92e64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335352}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0efeeff3-47fe-5165-905e-7ef041b1d387" :question/math-academy-id "q-65073" :question/problem "![](images/96/96b22799c75a4b19df4c6bddf8b63d11a7d0cab8a0cc481b8466a29eb1a2af15.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $tan⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/21/21170c11aa5ff0ab39b86f94588e5e8a0fa178d0c34b9aa1774ee9f07a4d0812.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{45}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{4}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{7}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{7})}{(\\frac{2}{7})}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{5}}{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259732 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0be5fee5-ff9c-5713-906a-6d90f5a9cb0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{54}}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256895} {:answer/id #uuid "15efda3b-3678-57d9-8d54-1db2e510c65d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256978} {:answer/id #uuid "26ace001-a008-507d-a0e9-16b26952290c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{154}}{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257114} {:answer/id #uuid "2c0cbc61-b93d-54e4-b926-1de5e2999404" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257176} {:answer/id #uuid "60c8fc36-e289-5c4e-a0d0-f7e4ddae3e65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{154}}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257633}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60c8fc36-e289-5c4e-a0d0-f7e4ddae3e65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{154}}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257633} :answer-field/id #uuid "80a8e351-07c7-5816-954b-d7240ccbef42" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259253}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "adb42fa3-fa6c-5e9a-800e-ffdd8192d291" :question/math-academy-id "q-124789" :question/problem "![](images/aa/aad0b38f5afb88c13039ac577a927e232d84905de40297ed824c966795ce7bb4.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{5},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $tan⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/e2/e2323a87ec4ab790714f4f6f5e5ff359495e93c6be5d76ae4b9f7ff15d14fd90.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{14}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{11}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{5}.$\n\nKnowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{5})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{5})}=\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{\\sqrt{11}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{\\sqrt{11}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{14}}{\\sqrt{11}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{\\sqrt{11}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{14\\cdot 11}}{11} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{154}}{11}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259733 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "148d9fa6-e709-5ed3-8dee-a9794d49d279" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256967} {:answer/id #uuid "57004998-41ca-5e4e-90f5-2c24561a08c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257531} {:answer/id #uuid "7ee702e6-7a9d-5c39-9f0f-93b8b0fe4698" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{35}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257913} {:answer/id #uuid "d244da59-6698-5edd-a3a0-8e822fc76895" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258615} {:answer/id #uuid "d255b826-a073-5d0c-8306-dc2845cf337e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258617}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d255b826-a073-5d0c-8306-dc2845cf337e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258617} :answer-field/id #uuid "8654efa3-cd29-5e20-bbaf-04bd03e93e30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259265}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dba2d6f5-ab4c-55d2-aafd-8aaa2c0dbeec" :question/math-academy-id "q-124791" :question/problem "![](images/c2/c2938adc4474cec0da06b5cee00b60110b7e7be86d2b0cec3f1cff99653b8f76.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5},$ what is $tan⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/af/af76b605cad900f45bc9def13e9ec2ffb12cb253ff0bdd4c07e764eac2a30937.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{12}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{13}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. Therefore $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5}.$\n\nKnowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{5})}{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{5})}=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}tan⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{13\\cdot 3}}{2\\cdot 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{39}}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259734 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "030abc43-15ab-542a-9efc-a5b8959e8901" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256806} {:answer/id #uuid "164f1f56-0609-51b8-a92f-5f6f8b2829eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256984} {:answer/id #uuid "b606a516-6e71-50b3-b37f-00403c1f1485" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258363} {:answer/id #uuid "bae703ab-5b1b-5842-8663-291050f98b09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258418} {:answer/id #uuid "f3d72a9c-545b-5d53-89de-2aadd8702aa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258913}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3d72a9c-545b-5d53-89de-2aadd8702aa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258913} :answer-field/id #uuid "e79ad56d-ed57-5258-b07c-74c9f1f477d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259438}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9926f7c6-9c2e-5943-b7e8-596de1b982b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-124796" :question/problem "![](images/ee/ee92f92a52d8ade2052a62a5c19906cd86027b47f22ec986a740b9379e66d8d1.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{1}{8},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "Any point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{1}{8}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{8}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{1}{8})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{1}{64}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{63}{64}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{8}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{3\\sqrt{7}}{8})}{(\\frac{1}{8})}=3\\sqrt{7}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335738 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07056c3e-506d-5641-9888-b4b318e25eff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333058} {:answer/id #uuid "a034f5c0-3b14-5e29-8def-edc95efbd80e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334397} {:answer/id #uuid "cb74b243-d73b-5089-bae4-f981f0a60cd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334760} {:answer/id #uuid "d2ffa90f-9b1f-53a0-9ae4-72f9fe2ebb09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334836} {:answer/id #uuid "d8504233-ad25-5121-8f69-909d3fab6b8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334884}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07056c3e-506d-5641-9888-b4b318e25eff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333058} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b238c5f-814a-5a46-a972-3a25a21b520c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335319}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8585ed26-d09c-5c8e-84bf-c2d2c03840ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-64730" :question/problem "![](images/2f/2f01b946a5c7c0f93c2171379603c4426913af5a15d0bc03434785144cfe4102.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},$ what is $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "Any point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{2}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{4}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. Therefore $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\nUsing the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we finally get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}=1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335789 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "194eaee8-54cb-563d-8f6d-d34f552da442" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333221} {:answer/id #uuid "8dcc27a4-b6ff-5196-bfc9-478bbd73a589" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334253} {:answer/id #uuid "a96bc338-b51a-5af0-adad-0e96975b2300" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334473} {:answer/id #uuid "ad3570c1-3d28-5add-bb53-9c8895491a2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334516} {:answer/id #uuid "e6417934-5eda-53e4-82d9-b2e434d37de6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334996}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad3570c1-3d28-5add-bb53-9c8895491a2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334516} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfce3d7a-f48e-534b-a3f3-76cd7412d6ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335631}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7581029a-054b-5d43-92f5-a2b561001b1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-65072" :question/problem "![](images/e6/e666ddc8a8eda452abd833e963446c96de5d34dfbf76e9c92564e85a48080524.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{3}{5},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $tan⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/fc/fc291f1fc5c4a712d3eef7ea2872de621855669d97445b24a2bc7a9da55949d9.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{3}{5}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{4}{5}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $tan⁡\\theta =\\frac{sin⁡\\theta }{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ntan⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{5})}{(\\frac{4}{5})}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335790 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b84d4ec-9f5f-5a47-8517-3bb8f8c9e6f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333094} {:answer/id #uuid "0f97daea-fc44-5b9a-b6c8-8ae1ec14226d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\sqrt{17}}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333127} {:answer/id #uuid "26887548-0fc3-51f2-9193-efc1f3262179" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333343} {:answer/id #uuid "9f0d2d95-0c48-5abf-8954-df6ad2d3263b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334390} {:answer/id #uuid "c3781df0-cce1-500d-ae05-5955c50dfe45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334685}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3781df0-cce1-500d-ae05-5955c50dfe45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334685} :answer-field/id #uuid "360fb473-e8de-57ee-bab2-0cdf46873b28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335346}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5aa7dee2-18db-5dfd-b251-d711e6634ce2" :question/math-academy-id "q-65075" :question/problem "![](images/d8/d846227996dd5fc1d6c3ca30108f548ac0d3743ec9262db0a551d28a32386996.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{9},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $cot⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/52/5284ae5a30a5d370d52c887cc6222ee4991a98d087dc751cbcfe5d7307ca1d47.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{9}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{9}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $sin⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $cos⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{9}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sin⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{9})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{17}{81}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{64}{81}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{8}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $sin⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{8}{9}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $cot⁡\\theta =\\frac{cos⁡\\theta }{sin⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\ncot⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{9})}{(\\frac{8}{9})}=\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{8}.\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Tangent or Cotangent Using the Pythagorean Identity"}]}
